    "body": "Image: © Kateryna Chyzhevska\nBy Alison Koo\n16 November 2026\nTaiwan liner operator Wan Hai’s re-entry into the transpacific market back in 2020, was a move that has paid off, as Asia-US East Coast is now the company’s largest revenue contributor, said chairman Chen Po-ting on Friday.\nSince 2020, Wan Hai has generated cumulative net profit of about TWD300bn ($9.11bn) through to Q3 26, despite recording a net loss of TWD5.8bn ($189m) during the market correction in 2023.\nMr Chen said: “We accelerated fleet expansion during the pandemic, taking a calculated risk, despite concerns among major shareholders that the market could return to normal once the health crisis subsided. We chose phased investments and continued monitoring market developments.”\nDespite concerns that the current massive orderbook for new box ships could depress rates next year, since Covid the liner shipping industry had moved from monopolistic competition into orderly rivalry, he added.\nFreight rates are no longer determined solely by supply and demand, with geopolitical developments, climate change, and logistical bottlenecks increasingly shaping the container shipping market.\nMr Chen asked: “Why must freight rates remain the same as before? Rising costs associated with green energy, environmental protection, and human rights are also changing the economics of shipping.”\nTo strengthen its long-haul routes, Wan Hai acquired second-hand ships and ordered larger vessels, and its capacity now stands at 627,456 teu, having grown by more than 132% since the pandemic, the second-largest increase among the world’s 12 biggest container shipping lines. The Taiwanese operator is the only major shipping line to operate a fully owned fleet, and does not charter any vessels.\nWan Hai will take delivery of 11 ships in 2027 – ten 16,000 teu vessels and one of 8,700 teu.\nThe additional capacity will support the deployment of larger vessels on routes serving the US east and west coasts, South America, the Middle East, India, and within Asia.\nCheck out today’s News in Brief podcast , on the latest developments in US tariffs and customs enforcement, what a Democrat-controlled Congress could mean for the US trade agenda – as well as air freight updates.",
